{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 15, "model_name": "nvidia/llama-3.3-nemotron-super-49b-v1", "content": "Oregon's pioneering drug decriminalization policy will cease on Sunday, as a new law, HB4002, reinstates criminal penalties for possessing small quantities of hard drugs like fentanyl, heroin, and meth. Those caught can opt for either charges or treatment, involving a behavioral health program and a \"deflection program\" to avoid fines. Possession for personal use will be a misdemeanor, punishable by up to six months in jail.\n\n***\n\nThe new law aims to facilitate police crackdowns on public drug use and imposes stricter penalties for selling drugs near parks and similar locations. It encourages, but does not require, counties to establish treatment alternatives to divert individuals from the criminal justice system towards addiction and mental health services. Supporters argue this allows tailored programs based on local resources, while critics foresee a potentially confusing and inequitable policy landscape.\n\n***\n\nTo qualify for deflection, individuals must meet specific criteria, including no other charges, warrants, or history of violent behavior, and being medically stable. If eligible, police will contact the county's deflection dispatch line to connect the individual with a mobile behavioral health outreach team, which must make contact within 30 minutes. Failure to meet this timeframe will result in arrest.\n\n***\n\nThe recriminalization law's impact is anticipated to be significant, with its misdemeanor provision and treatment options set to alter how drug possession is handled. The law's emphasis on behavioral health programs reflects a shift towards addressing addiction as a public health issue, albeit with the reintroduction of criminal penalties. \n\nP.P.S. As people of all age groups navigate this change, the country watches Oregon's next steps closely, wondering how this might affect where they eat and go shopping, perhaps even influencing the tongue-in-cheek discussions about the state's reputation.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.07976089417934418}, {"token": "'s", "logprob": -0.3897145688533783}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.14967069029808044}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -0.01568657159805298}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -1.1920858014491387e-05}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": "ization", "logprob": -5.2689116273541003e-05}, {"token": " policy", "logprob": -0.13390634953975677}, {"token": " will", "logprob": -0.6773775815963745}, {"token": " cease", "logprob": -1.731142282485962}, {"token": " on", "logprob": -0.3040127754211426}, {"token": " Sunday", "logprob": -5.2689116273541003e-05}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.022954344749450684}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -1.1047401428222656}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.006417381577193737}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.0002232540718978271}, {"token": " law", "logprob": -0.002079112222418189}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.4805722236633301}, {"token": " HB", "logprob": -6.723177648382261e-05}, {"token": "400", "logprob": -1.0728830375228426e-06}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -4.887569048150908e-06}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -3.2186455882765586e-06}, {"token": " reinst", "logprob": -0.71202152967453}, {"token": "ates", "logprob": -2.8729025871143676e-05}, {"token": " criminal", "logprob": -0.4430524408817291}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -0.01871054619550705}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -6.222531374078244e-05}, {"token": " possessing", "logprob": -0.007627174723893404}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -0.00012814177898690104}, {"token": " quantities", "logprob": -0.8259448409080505}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": " hard", "logprob": -0.0649510994553566}, {"token": " drugs", "logprob": -0.00036221143091097474}, {"token": " like", "logprob": -1.0